WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.572
Coding Exercise 2 changes the population rule.

00:00:03.572 --> 00:00:09.252
This time, every patient must appear, even if the patient has no admission.

00:00:09.252 --> 00:00:13.677
The phrase every patient tells you which side must be preserved.

00:00:13.677 --> 00:00:18.682
Patients should be on the left side of the join, and admissions should be optional.

00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:23.362
Before running the query, predict what an unmatched patient should look like.

00:00:23.362 --> 00:00:26.982
The patient ID and patient name should still have values.

00:00:26.982 --> 00:00:32.292
The admission ID and diagnosis should be NULL because no admission row matched.

00:00:32.292 --> 00:00:36.907
After Run and Check, find that unmatched patient in the result.

00:00:36.907 --> 00:00:41.977
Also notice that patients with multiple admissions still appear multiple times.

00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:48.062
LEFT JOIN preserves the patient population, but it does not force one row per patient.

00:00:48.062 --> 00:00:50.342
This is a useful distinction.

00:00:50.342 --> 00:00:54.977
Preservation and grain are related, but they are not the same thing.

00:00:54.977 --> 00:01:01.309
A LEFT JOIN can keep every patient and still return several rows for patients with several admissions.
